tel 1.0

base fulminate
import soundness

info   errors as values with structured messages: `Error`, `Message` and `m"…"`, `Panic`, `Diagnostics`
detail
    _Fulminate_ is the vocabulary of failure every Soundness library shares. An **error** is a
    value — a case class extending `Error` — carrying the typed fields which explain what went
    wrong and a **message** built from them; a `Message` is not a string but a structured
    value, written with the `m"…"` interpolator, which keeps each interpolated part distinct
    from the words around it so that the same message renders as plain text, styled terminal
    text, Markdown or HTML. Anything with a `Communicable` instance can be interpolated, which
    is how a type decides, once, how it reads in prose.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    case class PortError(port: Int)(using Diagnostics)
    extends Error(m"the port $port is not available")

    PortError(80).message.text   // t"the port 80 is not available"
    ```

    An error type has a number — `Error(405, 0)` is `SN-405` — which is stable across releases,
    never reused, and documented at `doc/errors/405.md`; its distinct causes are the cases of
    a `Reason` enum extending `Clarification`, each numbered within the error (`SN-405.1`), and
    each rendered through its own `Communicable`. A full error, grown step by step:

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label An error with a message
      code
          import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

          case class PortError(port: Int)(using Diagnostics)
          extends Error(m"the port $port is not available")

          PortError(80).getMessage   // the top-level package, then ": the port 80 is not available"
    frame
      label With a number, for the catalogue
      code
          import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

          case class PortError(port: Int)(using Diagnostics)
          extends Error(917, 0)(m"the port $port is not available")

          PortError(80).errorCode    // t"SN-917"
          PortError(80).getMessage   // "[↯SN-917] the port 80 is not available"
    frame
      label With a reason, numbered within it
      code
          import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

          object PortError:
            enum Reason(val number: Int) extends Clarification:
              case Taken      extends Reason(1)
              case Privileged extends Reason(2)

            object Reason:
              given communicable: Reason is Communicable =
                case Taken      => m"it is already in use"
                case Privileged => m"it is below 1024 and needs privilege"

          case class PortError(port: Int, reason: PortError.Reason)(using Diagnostics)
          extends Error(917, reason.number)(m"the port $port is not available because $reason")

          PortError(80, PortError.Reason.Privileged).errorCode   // t"SN-917.2"
          PortError(80, PortError.Reason.Privileged).message.text
          // t"the port 80 is not available because it is below 1024 and needs privilege"
    ##
    ```

    Every error is a `Hazard` — an `Exception` which is `caps.Pure` — so it can never carry a
    live capability out of the scope which confined it: an error holds the path which could
    not be opened, not the open handle. Whether an error captures a stack trace is a
    contextual choice, the `Diagnostics` given: `errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics`
    records one, and `errorDiagnostics.emptyDiagnostics` omits it, which is why every error's
    constructor takes `(using Diagnostics)`, and why an error which is expected and handled
    costs no more to build than any other value. A `Panic` is the one failure not meant to be
    handled: a `java.lang.Error` which says why a situation was believed impossible.

    For macros, `halt` and `warn` report a `Message` as a compile-time diagnostic, with the
    same `SN-` prefix, and a foreign `Throwable` is wrapped as an `Error.Unchecked` to make it
    a `Hazard`. How to *raise* and *handle* an error — `raises`, `Tactic`, `safely`,
    `recover`… — is `contingency`, which builds on this library.

    The library is two source sets. `fulminate.core` holds everything above and compiles for
    the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native; `fulminate.print` adds `printables.messagePrintable`,
    the `Printable` instance which lets a `Message` be printed, kept separate so that the
    error vocabulary does not depend on printing. Everything is exported to the `soundness`
    umbrella, and the `errorDiagnostics` and `printables` choice packages merge with the
    umbrella's own, so their givens are imported by name (`import
    errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics`): a wildcard import never imports givens.

refer fulminate.Error
refer fulminate.Message
refer fulminate.m
refer fulminate.Communicable
refer fulminate.Diagnostics
refer fulminate.errorDiagnostics
refer contingency.Tactic

entry .Error   the base of every Soundness error: a number, a structured message and optional diagnostics
  detail
      An error is a case class extending `Error`, carrying as fields the values which explain
      it, with its message written with `m"…"` from those same fields, so that the message
      can never describe a state the value is not in. The primary constructor is
      `Error(d, e)(message, cause)(using Diagnostics)`, with two shorter forms: `Error(d,
      e)(message)` and `Error(message, cause = null)`, the last for an error with no number.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      case class PortError(port: Int)(using Diagnostics)
      extends Error(m"the port $port is not available")

      val error = PortError(80)
      error.message.text      // t"the port 80 is not available"
      error.component         // the package the error class is in
      error.getStackTrace.nn.length > 0   // true, under stackTracesDiagnostics
      ```

      `d` is the error's number, a three-digit code unique across every Soundness library:
      it is allocated when the error type is written, never reused (a retired number's page
      stays, saying what became of it), and documented at `doc/errors/<d>.md` with its cause
      and the usual remedies, so that `SN-405` in a user's report can be looked up rather
      than guessed at. `e` is the number of the error's reason — the `number` of its `Reason`
      case, an enum extending `Clarification` — or 0 when the error has no reasons. An error
      written `Error(message)` has `d = 0`: no code, and `getMessage` prefixes its component
      instead. The code renders in the `[↯SN-ddd.e]` prefix of `#labelled`, of `getMessage`
      and of a compile-time `halt`.

      An `Error` is `caps.Pure`: it may never hold a live capability, because `throw` is the
      one channel capture checking cannot see, and an exception caught outside a scope
      arrives with its captures erased. The `Diagnostics` given decides whether the
      constructor captures a stack trace (`Exception`'s `writableStackTrace` is set from it),
      which is the one piece of ceremony defining an error imposes. The message text is also
      the exception's `getMessage`, so an error which does escape as a thrown exception still
      reads properly.

      The house style for the message is `doc/standards/messages.md`: lowercase, no trailing
      period, present tense, the specific resource at fault interpolated as a typed value,
      and never *invalid* or *bad* as the whole predicate. Errors are meant to be matched on:
      a handler deconstructs the case class and its `Reason`, never its text.
  refer fulminate.Message
  refer fulminate.Clarification
  refer fulminate.Diagnostics
  refer fulminate.Hazard
  refer fulminate.Error.Unchecked
  entry .apply   any `Throwable` as an `Error`: itself if it is one, else wrapped as `Error.Unchecked`
      `Error(throwable)` passes an `Error` through unchanged and wraps any other `Throwable`
      in an `Error.Unchecked`, which keeps its message and stack trace. This is how a JDK
      exception becomes a `Hazard` which can be raised.
  entry #d   the error's number: the `ddd` of `SN-ddd`, or 0 for an unnumbered error
  entry #e   the reason's number within the error: the `e` of `SN-ddd.e`, or 0
  entry #message   the error's message, as a structured `Message`
  entry #diagnostics   the `Diagnostics` the error was built with
      Available so that a translated error can be built with the original's choice:
      `InputError(text)(using error.diagnostics)`.
  entry #fullClass   the error class's fully-qualified name, as its segments
  entry #className   the error class's simple name
  entry #component   the first segment of the class's name: the library which defines it
      The first segment of the class's fully-qualified name, which is the top-level package
      for a Soundness error. It is the prefix of `getMessage` for an error with no number, so
      a message such as `galilei: the path is not a directory` says where the error came from.
  entry #errorCode   the code as text, `SN-ddd` or `SN-ddd.e`; empty for an unnumbered error
  entry #colourCode   the code as `[↯SN-ddd.e]`, with ANSI colours; empty for an unnumbered error
  entry #labelled   the message with its code prefixed: `[↯SN-ddd.e] message`
      The message alone when the error has no number. This is what `HaltTactic` reports when
      an error becomes a compile error.

  entry .Unchecked   a foreign `Throwable` wrapped as an `Error`, so that it can be raised
    detail
        Error 117: _unchecked error with message, `text`_, or _unchecked error with no
        message_. `Unchecked(throwable)` keeps the original's message as `#text` and
        adopts its stack trace, and is built with `emptyDiagnostics`, since the trace it carries
        is the original's. `Error(throwable)` is the usual way to make one: it passes a real
        `Error` through and wraps anything else. The original itself is not kept — `getCause`
        is `null` — only its message and stack trace, so a handler which meets one should
        surface it as a typed error, or fix the source of the throw, rather than match on the
        original's class.
    refer fulminate.Error
    entry .describe   the message for an original message text; _with no message_ when it is empty
    entry .apply   wraps a `Throwable`, keeping its message and stack trace
    entry #text   the original's message, or empty text
    entry #stackTrace   the original's stack trace, as the JVM array

entry .Hazard   the domain of raisable values: any pure `Exception`
    `type Hazard = Exception & caps.Pure`. `Error` is pure by inheritance, so every Soundness
    error is a `Hazard`; a JDK exception must be wrapped (by `Error(throwable)`, as an
    `Error.Unchecked`) to be raised, since purity cannot be established for an arbitrary
    `Throwable` subclass. Every `Tactic[error]`, `raises error` and `Attempt[_, error]` bounds
    its error by `Hazard`.

entry .Message   a structured message: fixed text with typed, distinguishable parts embedded
  detail
      A `Message` is `texts` — the literal pieces — interleaved with `messages`, the embedded
      parts, one fewer than the texts, so that the boundary between the fixed and the
      variable parts of a message survives to the point of rendering. `m"…"` builds one;
      `#text` flattens it to plain text; `#colorText` renders the embedded parts in italics on
      a terminal; a renderer for Markdown, HTML or styled text walks `#segments` or `#fold`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val name = t"world"
      val message = m"hello $name"

      message.texts      // List(t"hello ", t"")
      message.messages   // List(Message(t"world"))
      message.text       // t"hello world"
      (message + m"!").text   // t"hello world!"
      ```

      A message's parts may be nested to any depth: an embedded `Message` keeps its own
      structure, and `m"…"` also embeds the text between backticks as a part, so `m"the
      field `name` is absent"` renders `name` as a distinguishable part. `Message` is
      `Communicable` (as itself), so a message interpolates into another, and a `List[Message]`
      interpolates as bullets. Printing a `Message` needs `printables.messagePrintable`, from
      `fulminate.print`.
  refer fulminate.m
  refer fulminate.Communicable
  refer fulminate.printables
  entry .apply   a message of one literal text
      `Message(text)` is a message with no embedded parts. The overload `Message[tuple]
      (messages, done)` is the inline helper the `m"…"` macro uses to convert a tuple of
      substitutions through their `Communicable` instances; it is not for direct use.
  entry .communicable   a `Message` interpolates into another as itself
      In `Message`'s own companion so that it is in implicit scope for `Message is
      Transcribable to Message`, which is how a logger can log a bare message.
  entry #texts   the literal pieces, one more than the embedded parts; empty text where parts abut
  entry #messages   the embedded parts, in order
  entry #+   `left + right`: concatenation, with the texts at the join merged into one piece
  entry #segments   the message as one flat list of its texts and parts, interleaved
  entry #fold   renders the message depth-first, the function receiving each text with its nesting level
      `message.fold(initial)((accumulator, text, level) => …)`: every literal piece of the
      message and of its parts, in reading order, with the depth it is embedded at (0 for the
      top level). `#text` and `#colorText` are folds.
  entry #text   the message flattened to plain text, with multi-line literals unwrapped
      Lines of a triple-quoted `m"""…"""` are trimmed and joined with single spaces, and a
      blank line becomes a newline, so a message may be laid out across lines in source
      without the layout reaching the reader.
  entry #colorText   the message as terminal text, embedded parts in italics and deeper parts in bold italics
      ANSI styling only — no colour, despite the name — with depth 1 in italics and depth 2 or
      more in bold italics. A macro's `halt` uses it when the compiler's colour setting is on.
  entry #unwrap   collapses a multi-line string: lines trimmed and joined with spaces, blank lines as newlines
      The normalisation `#text` applies; public so that a renderer can apply the same rule
      to a literal.

entry .m   the `m"…"` interpolator: a `Message` from literal text and `Communicable` substitutions
    `m"the port $port is not available"` builds a `Message` whose literal pieces are the
    text between substitutions and whose parts are the substitutions, each converted through
    its `Communicable` instance — so only a value which knows how to read in prose can be
    interpolated, and a value with no instance is a compile error. A `Message` interpolates
    as itself, `Text` as itself (quoted with `“ ”` if empty or padded), the primitives as
    their decimal or character text, any `Textualizable` through its text, and a
    `List[Message]` as bullets.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val port = 80
    val reason = m"it needs privilege"

    m"the port $port is not available: $reason".text
    // t"the port 80 is not available: it needs privilege"

    m"the field `name` is absent".messages   // List(Message(t"name"))
    ```

    Text between a pair of backticks becomes an embedded part — a message of its own,
    which may itself contain substitutions — so an identifier or a literal fragment in the
    prose is distinguishable from the words around it, and renders in italics on a
    terminal or as code in Markdown. An unmatched backtick is a compile error, _the m""
    interpolator has an unmatched backtick_. The escapes `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\f`, `\b`,
    `\e`, `\\`, `\"`, `\'` and `\uXXXX` are decoded at compile time, and any other escape is
    a compile error. A triple-quoted `m"""…"""` may span lines; `Message#text` unwraps them.

    The house style for what a message says is `doc/standards/messages.md`.

entry .Communicable   the typeclass of values which can read in prose: a value to a `Message`
  detail
      `value is Communicable` converts a value to a `Message`, and is what `m"…"` asks of
      each substitution. A type which appears in messages gives an instance in its companion,
      usually derived from its text rendering, so that every message about such values agrees
      on how one reads; a `Reason` enum gives one which phrases each case. The instances in
      the companion cover `Text` (quoted with `“ ”` when empty or padded, so an absent or
      whitespace value is visible), `String`, `Char`, `Int`, `Long`, `Message`, any
      `Textualizable`, a `List[Message]` (as bullets, one per line), a `Specializable` (`Int`
      as `int`, for messages naming a primitive type), and — inside a macro, with `Quotes` in
      scope — a `Term` or an `Expr`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      enum Weekday:
        case Monday, Tuesday

      object Weekday:
        given communicable: Weekday is Communicable =
          case Monday  => m"the first day of the week"
          case Tuesday => m"the second day of the week"

      m"today is ${Weekday.Monday}".text   // t"today is the first day of the week"
      ```

      A `Communicable` is a `Transcribable to Message`: converting a value to a message is
      exactly how a loggable event is transcribed onto a log's carrier, which is what lets a
      logger log any `Communicable` event with no further bridge. `value.communicate` is the
      conversion as an extension method.
  refer fulminate.m
  refer fulminate.Message
  refer fulminate.communicate
  entry .text   `Text` as itself, quoted with `“ ”` if it is empty or begins or ends with a space
  entry .string   `String` as its text
  entry .char   a `Char` as the character
  entry .int   an `Int` in decimal
  entry .long   a `Long` in decimal
  entry .textualizable   any `Textualizable` value, as its text
  entry .term   a `quotes.reflect.Term`, as the compiler shows it; for macros, with `Quotes` in scope
  entry .expr   an `Expr[T]`, as the compiler shows it; for macros, with `Quotes` in scope
  entry .specializable   a primitive's companion (`Int`, `Boolean`…) as its lowercase name
      For a message naming a primitive type, as `Number.Error`'s _is not a valid int_ does.
  entry .listMessage   a `List[Message]` as bullets, one message per line
      Each message on its own line prefixed with ` - `, so an error which aggregates others
      says how many and lists them beneath. Do not join messages with commas by hand.
  entry #Result   `Message`
  entry #message   the message for a value
  entry #record   the message for a value: the `Transcribable` name for the same conversion
  entry #contramap   a `Communicable` for another type, by mapping it to this one
      `Communicable.text.contramap(_.tt)` is how the `String` instance is made.

entry .communicate   `value.communicate`: the value as a `Message`, through its `Communicable`

entry .Clarification   the trait a `Reason` enum extends: a number for each distinct cause of an error
    `trait Clarification { def number: Int }`. An error's distinct causes are the cases of a
    `Reason` enum extending `Clarification`, each with a number unique within the error, so
    that `Error(405, reason.number)` renders as `SN-405.1`, `SN-405.2`… and each has a
    sub-section of the error's page in `doc/errors`. A `Reason` carries data of its own where
    the diagnosis needs it (`Ipv4ByteOutOfRange(byte: Int)`), and gives its own
    `Communicable` instance phrasing each case, which the error's message interpolates. A
    `halt` or `warn` given a `Clarification` puts its number in the diagnostic's prefix.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    object Port:
      enum Reason(val number: Int) extends Clarification:
        case Taken      extends Reason(1)
        case Privileged extends Reason(2)

    Port.Reason.Privileged.number   // 2
    ```

entry .Diagnostics   whether an error captures a stack trace: `Diagnostics.capture` or `Diagnostics.omit`
  detail
      An opaque type (exported from `fulminate.internal`) with two values. Every error's
      constructor takes one as a contextual parameter and passes `captureStack` to
      `Exception`'s `writableStackTrace`, so an error built under `omit` has no stack trace
      and costs no more than any other value, which is what makes typed errors affordable in
      hot paths where they are expected and handled. The tactics which discard their errors
      (`safely`, `recover`) supply `omit` themselves; those which keep them take the
      `Diagnostics` in scope; a throwing tactic uses `capture`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class PortError(port: Int)(using Diagnostics)
      extends Error(m"the port $port is not available")

      locally:
        given Diagnostics = Diagnostics.omit
        PortError(80).getStackTrace.nn.length   // 0

      locally:
        given Diagnostics = Diagnostics.capture
        PortError(80).getStackTrace.nn.length > 0   // true
      ```

      The usual way to choose is importing one of the `errorDiagnostics` givens by name.
  refer fulminate.errorDiagnostics
  refer fulminate.Error

entry .errorDiagnostics   the two `Diagnostics` choices, as givens to import by name
  detail
      `import soundness.*` brings neither in, since a wildcard import never imports givens;
      one is imported by name wherever errors are constructed outside a tactic's thunk — a
      test, a `mitigate` case, a direct `PortError(80)`. Inside `abort(…)` and `raise(…)` the
      tactic supplies its own, so a `raises` method needs no import.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      ```
  refer fulminate.Diagnostics
  entry .emptyDiagnostics   `Diagnostics.omit`: no stack traces; cheaper where an error is expected and handled
  entry .stackTracesDiagnostics   `Diagnostics.capture`: stack traces, for debugging

entry .Panic   an error not meant to be caught: a programmer's understanding was wrong
  detail
      A `java.lang.Error` (not an `Exception`, and so not a `Hazard`) carrying a `Message`
      which says why the situation was believed impossible. Reaching one is not a failure to
      handle: it appears in no `raises` clause, and no tactic catches it. `panic(message)`
      throws one; `Panic(exception)` wraps an exception which was unexpectedly thrown, with a
      message naming its class and text.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def denominator(n: Int): Int =
        if n == 0 then panic(m"a zero denominator was checked for earlier") else n
      ```
  refer fulminate.panic
  entry .apply   a panic for an exception which was unexpectedly thrown
      `Panic(exception)` has the message _an `ClassName` exception was unexpectedly thrown;
      the error was: `text`_, with `[null]` for an exception without a message.
  entry #message   why the situation was believed impossible

entry .panic   throws a `Panic` with a message: the situation was believed impossible
    `panic(m"…")` has type `Nothing`, so it stands anywhere a value is needed — the
    unreachable branch of a match, the `else` of a check which an earlier step guaranteed.
    It is not an error to handle, and is not declared in a signature.

entry .halt   reports a compile error from a macro and aborts the expansion
    For macro implementations, with `Quotes` in scope. `halt(message)` reports the
    `Message`'s text (coloured, through `Message#colorText`, when the compiler's `-color`
    setting is on) with `report.errorAndAbort`; `halt(message, position)` attaches a
    position. The numbered overloads prefix the diagnostic with the error's code as
    `[↯SN-ddd] ` — `halt(d, message)`, `halt(d, message, position)` — or with its reason's
    number as `[↯SN-ddd.e] `: `halt(d, reason, message)` and `halt(d, reason, message,
    position)`, where `reason` is a `Clarification`. The position parameter of the numbered
    forms is `Matchable`, and a value which is not a `quotes.reflect.Position` (or `null`)
    reports without one. Every macro-raised code has a page in `doc/errors`, like a runtime
    error's.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import scala.quoted.*

    def check(expr: Expr[Int])(using Quotes): Expr[Int] = expr.value match
      case Some(n) if n > 0 => expr
      case _                => halt(m"the argument $expr must be a positive literal")
    ```

    `HaltTactic`, from `contingency`, turns a raised `Error` into a `halt` of its `labelled`
    message, so a parser which raises at run time reports the same error at compile time.

entry .warn   reports a compile warning from a macro
    For macro implementations, with `Quotes` in scope. The same overloads as `halt` —
    `warn(message)`, `warn(message, position)`, `warn(d, message)`, `warn(d, message,
    position)`, `warn(d, reason, message)` and `warn(d, reason, message, position)` — with
    `report.warning` in place of `report.errorAndAbort`, so the expansion continues.

entry .TextEscapes   decodes the standard escape sequences in text: `\n`, `\t`, `\uXXXX`…
  detail
      The escape rules `m"…"` and the other interpolators share, as runtime functions for a
      parser which meets escapes in its input. `escape(text)` decodes every escape in a text;
      `standardEscape` is one step of that loop. The recognised escapes are `\\`, `\n`, `\r`,
      `\f`, `\b`, `\t`, `\e`, `\"`, `\'` and `\uXXXX`. These functions throw
      `TextEscapes.Error` as a checked exception (`throws`, not `raises`), so a caller needs a
      `CanThrow[TextEscapes.Error]` or `unsafeExceptions.canThrowAny` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import unsafeExceptions.canThrowAny

      TextEscapes.escape(t"a\\tb")   // t"a\tb", with a real tab
      ```
  entry .standardEscape   decodes one character at a position: the character (or -1), the next position, and whether an escape is open
      `standardEscape(text, cursor, escaping)` reads the character at `cursor` in the state
      `escaping` says, and returns the decoded character (`-1` when the character opened an
      escape and produced nothing), the position to continue from, and the new state.
  entry .escape   decodes every escape in a text
      Throws `TextEscapes.Error` for an unrecognised escape, an incomplete `\u` sequence, or
      a text ending in a lone backslash.
  entry .Error   raised when an escape sequence is not valid
    detail
        Error 421: _the character `c` should not be escaped_, _the unicode escape is
        incomplete_ or _the final character cannot be an escape_. Thrown by `escape` and
        `standardEscape`.
    entry #initMessage   the message describing the invalid escape

entry .printables   the `Printable` instance for `Message`, as a given to import by name
  detail
      In `fulminate.print`, a separate source set, so that the error vocabulary every module
      depends on does not also depend on printing. The given is not in `Message`'s implicit
      scope from there, so it must be imported; `Printable` has no generic fallback, so a
      call site which forgets fails to compile rather than printing something degraded.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import printables.messagePrintable

      val message: Message = m"the port `80` is not available"
      message.print   // t"the port 80 is not available"
      ```
  entry .messagePrintable   prints a `Message` as its plain `text`, whatever the terminal

entry .internal   the home of `Diagnostics` and of the `m"…"` macro
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. `Diagnostics` is declared here, as an opaque type over `Boolean`,
      and exported to the `fulminate` package; the macro behind `m"…"` and its helper live
      alongside it.
  entry .Diagnostics   the opaque type `Diagnostics`; see `fulminate.Diagnostics`
    detail
        Declared here and exported; its page under the `fulminate` package has the account.
    entry .capture   errors record their stack trace
    entry .omit   errors record no stack trace
  entry .captureStack   an extension on `Diagnostics`: whether a stack trace is to be captured
      The `Boolean` passed to `Exception`'s `writableStackTrace` by `Error`'s constructor.
  entry .mSubMessages   converts a substitution, or a tuple of them, to messages through `Communicable`
  entry .mMacro   the macro behind `m"…"`: decodes escapes, splits on backticks, and splices a `Message`
